<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/foundational/training-load-adaptation">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson is the doorway into Sports Science. You need nothing from earlier lessons —
    only a body and some experience of exercise. Everything the whole subject stands on
    (one curve, and the habits of mind it generates) is built here.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain supercompensation: why a training session makes you worse before recovery makes you better, and why timing decides everything;</li>
    <li>define health and fitness precisely, and describe how the two relate without being the same thing;</li>
    <li>name the body's three energy systems and say which fuels they burn and when;</li>
    <li>list the health- and skill-related components of fitness, and explain why specificity makes "get fitter" too vague to program;</li>
    <li>choose sensible test protocols, and give the five reasons fitness testing exists;</li>
    <li>apply the principles of training — overload, progression, specificity, reversibility, variation, individuality — through the FITT levers;</li>
    <li>match each method of training to the component it buys, and sketch a beginner's week that respects the recovery curve.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever training appears, we ask where the recovery went. Load without
    recovery is not training; it is just damage on a schedule.
  </p>

  <h2>The strangest thing about exercise</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the strangest thing about exercise: the training session itself makes you
    <em>worse</em>, not better. Straight after a hard run you are slower, weaker and stiffer
    than before you started. What makes you better is what happens afterwards — during the
    days of eating, sleeping and easy movement, the body does not merely repair the damage;
    it over-rebuilds, preparing for the next assault by raising the ceiling. That over-rebuild
    is <strong>supercompensation</strong>, and it is the single idea behind every training
    plan ever written. Training doesn't make you fitter. <strong>Training plus
    recovery</strong> makes you fitter — the exercise is only the stimulus, the way a
    question is only the stimulus for an answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The timing turns out to matter as much as the effort. Your body is a system that adapts
    to stress it survives, and it adapts <em>during</em> the rest, not the work. So the whole
    of programming — when to train hard, when to rest, when to test — is the art of timing
    the next session onto the peak of the last one's recovery curve. Get the timing wrong in
    either direction and the same work produces either injury or nothing.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="one session, plotted — the dip is fatigue; the bump above baseline is adaptation; the crossings mark the window" widget="plotter" config={superCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the marked crossings are where the green curve crosses
    baseline: about <strong>1–2 days of fatigue</strong>, then a window of elevated capacity
    around days 3–5. Train again too early and you stack fatigue onto fatigue (the road to
    overtraining); too late and the adaptation has faded; just right and each wave starts
    higher than the last. Worked example: a novice runner does a hard 5 km on Monday.
    Tuesday she feels leaden — that is the dip. Thursday, the same pace feels lighter — that
    is the peak. If she races on Tuesday and jogs on Sunday, she has used the curve
    backwards twice in one week.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one curve — dip, rebuild, peak — explains why plans rest,
    progress and deload. Before we program against the curve, we need to agree on what the
    curve is even buying: two words, health and fitness, that sound like synonyms and are
    not.
  </p>

  <h2>Health, fitness — and how they differ</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus starts by separating two words people use interchangeably.
    <strong>Health</strong> is a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being —
    a whole-person condition, not a scoreboard. <strong>Fitness</strong> is narrower: the
    ability to meet the demands of an environment or a task. A sumo wrestler is
    extraordinarily fit for his sport and may have hypertension; a marathon runner can have
    a racing heart rate and still be depressed. Fitness is task-shaped and partly
    negotiable; health is whole-person and not.
  </p>
  <p>
    The relationship between the two is positive but not identical: more fitness generally
    improves health (regular activity cuts cardiovascular-disease risk roughly a third,
    improves mood, sleep and insulin sensitivity), but you can chase fitness in ways that
    damage health — under-eating, overtraining, doping — and you can support health while
    remaining untrained. The practical target is fitness <em>for your life</em>: enough
    capacity that the demands of your days do not exceed you.
  </p>

  <h2>Diet and energy sources: the fuel side of adaptation</h2>
  <p>
    Adaptation is construction, and construction needs materials. The body runs on three
    fuel systems. For the first ~10 seconds of all-out effort, the <strong>ATP–phosphocreatine
    system</strong> powers sprints and lifts. From roughly 10 seconds to 2 minutes, the
    <strong>anaerobic (lactic) system</strong> burns glucose without oxygen, producing the
    burn of a 400 m race. Beyond that, the <strong>aerobic system</strong> burns
    carbohydrate and fat <em>with</em> oxygen — the system that powers everything from a
    5 km run to sitting in this lesson. Carbohydrate is the high-output fuel (glycogen
    stored in muscle and liver); fat is the vast low-output reserve; protein is mostly a
    building material, not a fuel.
  </p>
  <p>
    Timing follows function: endurance athletes replenish glycogen with carbohydrate in the
    hours after long sessions (the "window" is real but generous — hours, not minutes),
    while strength training drives a need for protein spread across the day, roughly 1.4–2.0
    g per kg of body mass for athletes in hard training. Chronic under-fuelling — the trap
    called RED-S, relative energy deficiency in sport — does the opposite of adaptation:
    stalled progress, lost periods in women, stress fractures and immune suppression. You
    cannot supercompensate on an empty site.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the lactic acid from a hard session sits in the muscles
  and causes next-day soreness. It is an appealing story — the burn was there, the soreness
  came later, cause and effect. But lactate — its proper name — is largely cleared or
  consumed as fuel within about an hour of stopping; the soreness arriving a day or two
  later comes from the repair processes around micro-damage, which is why it tracks novelty
  to the muscle rather than anything left over from the burn. Lactate is less a poison than
  a shuttle, and endurance training teaches the body to reuse it.</p>

  <h2>Components of fitness: what exactly are we adapting?</h2>
  <p>
    "Get fitter" is too vague to program. Training science splits fitness into components,
    each with its own adaptation curve. The <strong>health-related components</strong> are
    cardiovascular endurance (the heart–lung delivery system, measured by VO₂max), muscular
    strength (maximum force), muscular endurance (repeated submaximal effort), flexibility
    (range of motion at joints) and body composition (the fat-to-lean ratio). The
    <strong>skill-related components</strong> — agility, balance, coordination, power, speed
    and reaction time — matter most in sport. The point of the list is the principle of
    <strong>specificity</strong>: each component adapts to its own stimulus, so a plan must
    name which components it is buying. Long slow running buys cardiovascular endurance and
    barely touches strength; heavy squatting buys strength and barely touches VO₂max.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "muscle turns to fat" (or fat to muscle). It feels true
  because athletes visibly soften when they stop. But they are different tissues; one
  cannot become the other. What actually happens is simpler — training lapses, muscle
  shrinks from disuse while fat accumulates from unchanged eating. Two processes, wrongly
  narrated as one transformation.</p>

  <h2>Fitness testing: protocols and why we bother</h2>
  <p>
    You cannot manage a curve you never measure, which is why testing is part of training,
    not an interruption to it. Standard <strong>test protocols</strong> exist for each
    component: the multi-stage fitness test (beep test) and Cooper 12-minute run estimate
    aerobic endurance; the vertical jump or broad jump estimate power; the handgrip
    dynamometer and 1-repetition-maximum tests measure strength; the sit-and-reach measures
    flexibility; skinfolds or bioelectrical impedance estimate body composition.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>reasons for testing</strong> go beyond curiosity. Tests (1) establish
    baselines, so training loads can be set as percentages of what you can actually do; (2)
    track progress, turning adaptation from a feeling into a number; (3) reveal weaknesses
    that limit performance — a sprinter with poor flexibility is a hamstring tear waiting to
    happen; (4) motivate, because plotted improvement is the most powerful adherence drug
    known to sport; and (5) help talent identification and goal-setting. The rules that make
    a test meaningful: validity (it measures the component it claims to), reliability (same
    conditions, same result), and sensible protocol — warm up, standardise the conditions,
    and never schedule a max test the day after your hardest session, or you will measure
    your fatigue and call it your fitness.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the curve tells us adaptation happens in recovery; the
    components tell us what is adapting; the tests tell us where the curve currently sits.
    The principles of training are simply the curve turned into rules.
  </p>

  <h2>Principles of training and overload</h2>
  <p>
    The supercompensation curve generates the classic training principles as corollaries.
    <strong>Progressive overload</strong>: the stimulus must exceed what the body is used
    to, and must keep growing as you adapt — the same 20 press-ups that built you at week
    one maintain you by week ten. Overload can be raised by frequency, intensity, time or
    type (the <strong>FITT</strong> levers). <strong>Specificity</strong>: you get what you
    train for, right down to the muscle fibres and energy systems recruited.
    <strong>Reversibility</strong>: use it or lose it — endurance adaptations begin to fade
    within two weeks of stopping, strength slower but surely. <strong>Variation</strong>
    (and its planned form, periodization): the stimulus must change over months, because a
    plateau is the body announcing it has finished adapting to today's question.
    <strong>Individuality</strong>: the same session produces different curves in different
    bodies — genetics sets the slope.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "no pain, no gain" — that soreness equals effectiveness.
  It persists because soreness is real and does follow hard sessions, so the correlation
  looks convincing. But delayed-onset muscle soreness is a rough marker of novelty to the
  muscle, not of growth, and chasing it pushes people to stack fatigue faster than they
  recover. Pain that is sharp, one-sided or in a joint is not training stimulus; it is a
  damage report.</p>

  <h2>Methods of training</h2>
  <p>
    Each method is a tool aimed at a component. <strong>Continuous training</strong> (steady
    running, cycling, swimming for 20+ minutes) builds the aerobic base. <strong>Fartlek</strong>
    — Swedish for "speed play" — mixes paces inside one continuous run, teaching the body to
    change gears. <strong>Interval training</strong> alternates work and relief with
    precision: 6 × 400 m fast with 90 s jog recoveries, for example, accumulates more
    high-quality time at high oxygen uptake than a flat tempo run. High-intensity interval
    training (HIIT) compresses this to short, near-maximal bursts and delivers aerobic gains
    in a fraction of the session time. <strong>Weight (resistance) training</strong> builds
    strength and power through progressive loading — typically 1–5 repetitions at heavy
    loads for strength, 8–12 for muscle growth, 15+ for muscular endurance.
    <strong>Plyometrics</strong> (jumping, bounding) train elastic power. <strong>Circuit
    training</strong> rotates stations to blend endurance and strength efficiently, and
    <strong>flexibility work</strong> — static stretching held 20–30 s, or dynamic mobility
    drills — maintains the range that lets the other methods run safely.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked programme sketch shows the principles meeting the methods: a beginner wants
    general fitness, so she trains four days a week — two continuous 30-minute runs (aerobic
    base), one full-body resistance session (strength), one interval session (VO₂max). Load
    rises about 10% per week, never 10% per day, because tendons and bone remodel slower
    than muscle and cardiovascular fitness — which is exactly why "too much, too soon"
    injures connective tissue first. Every fourth week drops back to consolidate: a planned
    dip that is simply the supercompensation curve drawn across weeks instead of days.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A friend trains hard every single day for three weeks and gets slower. Explain what is happening using the supercompensation curve.</li>
    <li>Which energy system dominates a 100 m sprint, a 400 m race, and a 10 km run — and what fuel does each burn?</li>
    <li>A coach tests an athlete's vertical jump the morning after the heaviest squat session of the year. The score is down. What measurement error has occurred?</li>
    <li>Why does "do 20 press-ups a day forever" violate the principle of progressive overload?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Each new session lands on the fatigue dip of the last
    instead of the adaptation peak, so fatigue stacks onto fatigue — this is
    non-functional overreaching heading toward overtraining. The fix is a recovery block,
    after which performance usually rebounds above where it stalled. (2) Sprint: ATP–PC
    system, phosphocreatine already stored in the muscle. 400 m: anaerobic (lactic) system
    burning glucose without oxygen. 10 km: aerobic system burning a mix of carbohydrate and
    fat with oxygen. (3) She measured fatigue, not fitness — a max test the day after the
    hardest session violates protocol. The test must be valid, reliable and taken in a
    recovered state, or the number is about yesterday's load rather than true capacity. (4)
    Once the body has adapted to 20 press-ups, the stimulus no longer exceeds what it is
    used to — the same work now <em>maintains</em> rather than builds. Overload must keep
    growing (more reps, slower tempo, added load) or the curve flattens.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Training is a stimulus; adaptation happens in recovery — supercompensation (dip, rebuild, peak) is the idea every plan is built on.</li>
    <li>Health is whole-person well-being; fitness is the ability to meet a task's demands — related, but chasing one can damage the other.</li>
    <li>Three fuel systems in series: ATP–phosphocreatine (about 10 s), anaerobic/lactic (10 s to 2 min, glucose), aerobic (beyond, carbohydrate and fat with oxygen).</li>
    <li>Fuelling is part of adaptation: carbohydrate after endurance work, protein spread through the day, and RED-S as the cost of chronic under-fuelling.</li>
    <li>Specificity rules everything: fitness has named components, each adapting only to its own stimulus — so a plan must name what it buys.</li>
    <li>Testing (beep test, jumps, dynamometry, sit-and-reach) exists to baseline, track, reveal weaknesses, motivate and select — and is meaningless without validity, reliability and a recovered athlete.</li>
    <li>The principles — progressive overload via FITT, specificity, reversibility, variation, individuality — are corollaries of the curve; "no pain, no gain" is not one of them.</li>
    <li>Methods map to components: continuous, fartlek, intervals, resistance, plyometrics, circuits, flexibility — and connective tissue remodels slowest, which is why ramping up too fast injures it first.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson gave you the curve and the rules it generates. The next lessons zoom into
    its axes: strength and hypertrophy (what overload does to muscle and nerve, and how the
    rep ranges really work), nutrition for performance (the fuel side in full), and later
