    plus testing and analytics at the elite edge. Every one of them is this page, drawn at
    higher magnification.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The plot above is a picture of Banister's <strong>fitness–fatigue model</strong>, the
    oldest formal account of training: every session leaves two after-effects, one negative
    (fatigue, decaying fast) and one positive (fitness, decaying slowly), and your
    performance is their difference. It explains at a stroke why tapering before competition
    works — cut the training load and fatigue evaporates within days while the slow-decaying
    fitness remains, so performance rises. The model is a simplification (real adaptations
    are dozens of processes with different time constants), but it remains the working
    mental model of every strength coach and the basis of "training-load" analytics in elite
    sport.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quantitatively, the timescales are well studied. Neural strength gains appear within
    2–4 weeks (better recruitment, before bigger fibres); muscle cross-section needs 6–8+
    weeks; VO₂max responds over 4–12 weeks of aerobic work; tendon and bone collagen
    remodels over months — hence tendon injuries presenting as the classic error of an
    enthusiastic first month. Detraining is asymmetric: VO₂max can fall ~5–10% within 2–4
    weeks of total rest, while strength persists longer and returns faster when retrained
    (muscle nuclei, once gained, appear to persist).
  </p>
  <p>
    The expert edge cases are about stacking. <strong>Functional overreaching</strong> is a
    deliberate, short overload block where fatigue accumulates for 1–2 weeks followed by a
    recovery peak — the curve used on purpose. <strong>Non-functional overreaching</strong>
    is the same thing done too long: weeks of stalled performance that weeks of rest can
    still fix. <strong>Overtraining syndrome</strong> is the endpoint: months of depression
    of mood, sleep, immunity and performance that rest alone may not reverse. The
    monitoring toolkit for staying on the right side of the line — morning resting heart
    rate, heart-rate variability, sleep quality, mood questionnaires — exists because the
    curve punishes greed first in the knees, shoulders and motivation.
  </p>

